---
/**
 * What each badge in the tracker means, and where the table's numbers come from.
 *
 * Its own component so `UpstreamTracker.astro` stays the table it is named for. The wording is
 * deliberate on one point: a closed row is not a rejected one, and saying so here is the only
 * place the page explains what GitHub's bare "Closed" actually meant.
 */
import { checkedOn, sourceClause } from "../lib/upstream-summary";
---

<p class="gmt-table-note">
  <strong>Already handled</strong>: GMT gives you the correct answer today, whichever
  polyfill version you have installed. <strong>Doesn't affect GMT</strong>: the
  bug is in code GMT doesn't use. <strong>Fix filed upstream</strong>: we have
  written the fix and the polyfill's maintainers have it; it reaches you with the
  next polyfill release. <strong>Reported upstream</strong>: we sent the
  maintainers the bug and a way to reproduce it. A row marked
  <strong>Theirs</strong> is a report someone else opened, where our part was the
  evidence rather than the fix. Each closed row says why it closed: so far every
  one was closed because the fix was going somewhere better, not because it was
  turned down. We read each report's state from GitHub every time this site is
  built (last checked {checkedOn} UTC{sourceClause}). <strong>Replies</strong>
  counts comments from the project's maintainers and other users.
</p>
